      <!-- H2 #1: Crown vs Cap -->
      <div class="space-y-4">
        <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-4xl font-serif text-japandi-charcoal font-medium tracking-tight">
          Crown vs Cap: Understanding the Terms
        </h2>
        <p class="text-sm sm:text-base text-japandi-earth font-light leading-relaxed">
          Patients frequently ask whether a dental cap is different from a dental crown. Clinically, they are exactly the same treatment. "Dental cap" is the traditional, colloquial term popularized because the restoration literally caps or covers the visible portion of a tooth. In modern restorative dentistry, clinicians use the term "dental crown" to describe a precision-engineered prosthetic restoration that covers 360 degrees of the prepared tooth down to the gum margin.
        </p>
        <p class="text-sm sm:text-base text-japandi-earth font-light leading-relaxed">
          Unlike simple surface fillings that sit inside an existing tooth cavity, a crown acts as a continuous structural helmet. It shields the underlying tooth structure from horizontal shear forces, biting pressure, and bacterial micro-leakage. Crowns are indicated when more than half of the natural tooth enamel has been lost to deep decay, repeated fillings, trauma, or endodontic intervention.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- H2 #2: Crown After Root Canal -->
      <div class="space-y-4">
        <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-serif text-japandi-charcoal font-medium tracking-tight">
          Crown After Root Canal: Protecting Weakened Teeth
        </h2>
        <p class="text-sm sm:text-base text-japandi-earth font-light leading-relaxed">
          Undergoing a root canal treatment removes the diseased nerve, blood supply, and lymphatics from within the pulp chamber. While this resolves infection and relieves pain, it leaves the remaining natural tooth dehydrated, brittle, and hollowed out. Chewing forces on molars and premolars regularly exceed 500 Newtons, which can easily cause an unprotected, root-treated tooth to split vertically down the middle.
        </p>
        <p class="text-sm sm:text-base text-japandi-earth font-light leading-relaxed">
          When a tooth fractures vertically below the bone level, it cannot be saved and requires extraction. Placing a high-strength CAD/CAM crown after root canal treatment distributes occlusal forces evenly across the entire root surface, preventing catastrophic breakage and ensuring your natural tooth foundation remains functional for decades. At Aureva Dental, we reinforce the tooth core with a bonded composite buildup before taking precision impressions for crown fabrication.
        </p>
      </div>

